        "id": 128462,
        "name": "Donner",
        "username": "Donner",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/donner/{size}/1903_2.png",
        "created_at": "2015-01-20T23:20:01.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I think that the best way to word something that would trigger twice (due to having two sources, like synaptic interruption), it should say \"Whenever X\" instead of \"the next X\" or \"the first X\".&nbsp; Then, if the first instance that would trigger, does, it uses the trigger the second would have used.&nbsp; And the second can then trigger when the event occurs again.&nbsp; But those usually require destruction of the card or some other way to signify that particular instance has triggered.&nbsp; If it is a card that stays in play but should redirect if X damage makes it through (only once each turn, though), how should it be worded so that two instances of that card can redirect two instances of that damage and no more?</p><p>Then, once we figure out how they should be worded, we can see if the current wording on any of these cards seems like they were aiming for that goal or for a single-shot regardless of instances effect.</p><p>Personally, I think the cards that say \"the first\" are trying to be multi-card, single instance (no matter how many cards of that type you have out, they only trigger once) and the ones that say \"the next\" are trying to be multi-card, multi-instance (each card stops one instance).&nbsp; In which case the flow order goes like this:&nbsp; Damage is trying to be dealt.&nbsp; Check the first card that says it can stop it.&nbsp; It triggers and is spent.&nbsp; The next time damage is dealt, we skip the first card and check the next.&nbsp; If it is unspent, it becomes spent and stops it.&nbsp; Otherwise, the wording difference between \"first\" and \"next\" has no point and they should all say first or all say next.</p>",
